WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:04.740
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:09.780
From many fond childhood memories, most of
us are quite fond of the colorful physical

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.190
appearance of bubbles and fuel-water mixtures.

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:17.740
We also know surprisingly little about this
peculiar phenomenon where the color of an

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:22.500
object changes when we either turn our head
or change the lighting.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:27.720
This happens in a very colorful manner, and
physicists like to call this iridescence or

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.119
goniochromism.

00:00:29.119 --> 00:00:33.650
What is even less known, is that if we try
to use a light simulation program to make

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:39.140
an image with leather, we'll be surprised
to see that it also shows a pronounced goniochromatic

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:40.140
effect.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:46.190
An even more less known fact is that quite
a few birds, insects, minerals, sea shells,

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:49.070
and even some fruits are iridescent as well.

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:53.480
I've added links to some really cool additional
readings to the video description for your

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:54.480
enjoyment.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:01:00.300
This effect is caused by materials that scatter
different colors of light in different directions.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:05.799
A white incoming light is therefore scattered
not in one direction, but in a number of different

00:01:05.799 --> 00:01:08.110
directions, sorted by their colors.

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:13.429
This is why we get these beautiful, rainbow-colored
patterns that we all love so much.

00:01:13.429 --> 00:01:18.539
Now that we know what iridescence is, the
next step is obviously to infuse our light

00:01:18.539 --> 00:01:21.810
simulation programs to have this awesome feature.

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:27.130
This paper is about simulating this effect
with microfacets, which are tiny microstructures

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:29.549
on the surface of rough objects.

00:01:29.549 --> 00:01:35.439
And with this, it is now suddenly possible
to put a thin iridescent film onto a virtual

00:01:35.439 --> 00:01:38.869
object and create a photorealistic image out
of it.

00:01:38.869 --> 00:01:43.420
If you are into math and would like to read
about some tasty spectral integration in the

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:47.609
frequency space with Fourier transforms, this
paper is for you.

00:01:47.609 --> 00:01:51.369
If you are not a mathematician, also make
sure to have a look because the production

00:01:51.369 --> 00:01:54.350
quality of this paper is through the roof.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:59.369
The methodology, derivations, comparisons
are all really crisp.

00:01:59.369 --> 00:02:00.369
Loving it.

00:02:00.369 --> 00:02:04.310
If you have a look, you will get a glimpse
of what it takes to create a work of this

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:05.310
quality.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:08.759
This is one of the best papers in photorealistic
rendering I've seen in a while.

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:13.400
In the meantime, I am getting more and more
messages from you Fellow Scholars who tell

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:18.670
their stories on how they chose to turn their
lives around and started studying science

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:20.630
because of this series.

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:21.630
Wow.

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:25.840
That's incredibly humbling, and I really don't
know how to express my joy for this.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:30.590
I always say that it's so great to be a part
of the future, and I am delighted to see that

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:35.500
some of you want to be a part of the future
and not only as an observer, but as a research

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:36.500
scientist.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:41.610
This sort of impact is stronger than the absolute
best case scenario I have ever dreamed of

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:42.610
for the series.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:46.720
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

